The `addAll` method in Java is used to add all elements from a collection to the current collection. It returns a boolean value indicating whether any elements were added to the collection. Here's a summary of the method:

```java
@Override
public boolean addAll(final Collection<? extends E> c) {
    Iterator<? extends E> i = c.iterator();
    boolean changed = false;
    while (i.hasNext()) {
        boolean ret = add(i.next());
        if (!changed) {
            changed = ret;
        }
    }
    return changed;
}
```

This method works as follows:
1. It takes a collection `c` as a parameter.
2. It creates an iterator `i` for the collection `c`.
3. It initializes a boolean variable `changed` to `false`.
4. It enters a `while` loop that continues as long as there are elements in the collection.
5. Inside the loop, it calls the `add` method on the current element of the collection and stores the result in the variable `ret`.
6. If `changed` is `false` (meaning no elements were added in the previous iteration), it sets `changed` to `ret`.
7. After the loop, it returns the value of `changed`, which indicates whether any elements were added to the collection.

